\f1\fs22 By afternoon, Hayley had exhausted the safer investigative options. Time to confront the predator directly. She couldn\'92t run it through channels\'97anything touching that address would be flagged and scrubbed before she reached the front door, and she didn\'92t trust a single colleague enough to bring them along. She attended Haloren Smythe\'92s address alone, and was surprised to find a decently presentable concierge at the greeting station in the lobby.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93May I help you?\'94 the concierge asked, sounding as if she genuinely meant it.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I\'92d kill for a decent cup of coffee,\'94 Hayley quipped.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Without missing a beat, the concierge replied, \'93There\'92s an excellent cafe above the Rail station. They do a blue cheese and Vegemite croissant that\'92s next level.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley hadn\'92t tasted one of those since before her grandmother died in a corner store supplier invoice dispute. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley smiled sheepishly and waved off the conversation. \'93No, thank you. I\'92m here to visit someone\'85 Haloren Smythe, apartment twenty-five\'85 oh\'85 sixty-four?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The concierge instantly tensed. Hayley always caught details like that. \'93Twenty-five sixty-four?\'94 the concierge repeated, her voice suddenly as strangled as a kookaburra with a snake caught sideways.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Uh-huh.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93One moment. I\'92ll call to let him know you\'92re here. May I have your name, please?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley flashed her badge. \'93Detective Superintendent Hayley Lanning.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Oh, you\'92re a TRAMP!\'94 Relief flooded the concierge\'92s face as she typed something unseen behind the desk\'97far more than four numbers, Hayley noticed.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Good morning, Mr Smythe? It\'92s Jody from downstairs. A Detective \'85 Hayley Lanning is here\'85 Yes\'85 Of course. I\'92ll tell her.\'94 She hung up. \'93Mr Smythe is out of town today. He says you can call his office to arrange an appointment.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley\'92s face hardened. \'93Please dial again. Twenty-five sixty-four.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Jody looked cornered but, hands shaking, followed instructions as Hayley circled to the business side of the counter. This time, Hayley watched Jody\'92s fingers: four digits on the wall intercom, not the desk console she\'92d hammered earlier. \'93Put it on speaker,\'94 Hayley said, reaching to press the right button herself when Jody hesitated.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The line connected. Silence, then a feminine voice responded.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Yes?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93It\'92s Jody, from Faircloud Apartments again,\'94 Jody squeaked.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>A pause, then: \'93Is there a problem?\'94 The tone was smooth\'97an operator used to \'93solving\'94 problems rather than denying them.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Jody twisted in place. \'93Your visitor is really quite\'85\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Amenable,\'94 Hayley prompted with a whisper.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Amenable,\'94 Jody echoed.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93To meeting you at your earliest convenience,\'94 Hayley whispered again.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Jody parroted. Another pause; then: \'93We\'92ll send a team. ETA five minutes. Stall her.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The line went dead. Jody\'92s composure collapsed. She snatched her bag, muttered, \'93No job is worth this taxing shit,\'94 and scrambled for the exit, nearly coming out of her shoes, leaving Hayley alone and an unlocked tablet behind.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley had perhaps five minutes. She made them count. In less than twenty seconds, she\'92d rifled the station\'92s cupboards and drawers, found the emergency maintenance keycard that would open anything in the building. Also in less than twenty seconds, a PAPER Tactical Response team scrambled, launching an armoured hover-copt into the air.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley strolled, not sprinted, to the lifts. Three arrived at once; she picked one. Within forty-five seconds, she rode up, found apartment twenty-five sixty-four, and let herself in. Ninety seconds, she guessed, to case the place.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Through the window, she never noticed the tiny black speck bearing down at full hover-copt speed.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Inside, the flat was decorated in unliveable creams and beiges. Empty. No lounge, no screens, not even light fittings. No trace anyone lived here. Still new paint, new floor sealant. She could leave on that knowledge alone, but her grandmother\'92s voice rang in her head\'97do the job properly\'97and Hayley, if nothing else, was thorough.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Outside, the hover-copt had already identified her infrared heat signature and the Tactical team had approval to arm.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The stasis unit and fridge were empty. So were cupboards, wardrobes (no clothes, no boxes), bedrooms (no beds). Kitchen drawers: nothing.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The hover-copt closed.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Bathrooms? Spotless, with builder\'92s packaging still on some fixtures, no soap, no toothbrush, not even a roll of toilet paper. She opened the vanity cabinet\'97empty shelves, a dust ring where a price sticker had been peeled. The shower tiles were yet to be grouted. The whole place looked like a display suite left out in the cold.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley moved from the master bedroom, into the corridor, back to the living room.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The hover-copt\'92s weapons strafed the apartment. Particle shots burst every window, and neatly perforated the plaster. In the kitchen, the chiller fridge wheezed as its door screens exploded, while the stasis unit did what it did best: it stayed inert beyond the conventional laws of physics.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The front door splintered and broke like kindling.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley\'92s body lay face-down in the hallway, hurled there as the windows burst inward.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>It was over. The hover-copt did a lap of the building, then set down in the forecourt. The Tactical Response team spilled out, certain their work was done.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>But Hayley\'92s body\'97on autopilot, mind in shock\'97scurried to the lift, jabbed the console, and collapsed inside as the doors shut. One thought punched through the ringing\'97basement, service dock, out\'97and her finger found the right button. She rode down, cowering in the corner, arms over her head. Like some nocturnal marsupial, Hayley\'92s body darted straight for the garbage room, used the keycard, and slipped out through the service dock into the hot stinking afternoon.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Much later, after Hayley\'92s body and mind finally reconciled, she would discover that garbage stench was, surprisingly, less nauseating than new apartment smell.
